          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "0001024",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "title": "Task 1 \u2014 Reproduce the Bug",
                "header_size": "h3",
                "title_color": "#14151A"
              },
              "elements": []
            },
            {
              "id": "0001025",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "<p>Run the current dashboard query (starter/dashboard_query.sql, provided as-is, don't edit it) and compare its row count to the number of distinct orders. The dashboard returns more rows than there are orders, and a noticeably higher total revenue. Confirm this for yourself before moving on.</p>",
                "text_color": "#54575F"
              },
              "elements": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "0001029",
          "elType": "container",
          "isInner": true,
          "settings": {
            "flex_direction": "column",
            "flex_gap": {
              "unit": "px",
              "size": 10
            },
            "padding": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "20",
              "right": "20",
              "bottom": "20",
              "left": "20",
              "isLinked": true
            },
            "background_background": "classic",
            "background_color": "#FFFFFF",
            "border_border": "solid",
            "border_color": "#E7E3D9",
            "border_radius": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "16",
              "right": "16",
              "bottom": "16",
              "left": "16",
              "isLinked": true
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "0001027",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "title": "Task 2 \u2014 Find the Grain Problem",
                "header_size": "h3",
                "title_color": "#14151A"
              },
              "elements": []
            },
            {
              "id": "0001028",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "<p>Query orders directly. Some order_ids appear 2 or 3 times, look at their status, updated_at and order_version. The table isn't at one-row-per-order grain; it's at one-row-per-status-update grain, and that's exactly what fans out when it's joined to order_items.</p>",
                "text_color": "#54575F"
              },
              "elements": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "000102c",
          "elType": "container",
          "isInner": true,
          "settings": {
            "flex_direction": "column",
            "flex_gap": {
              "unit": "px",
              "size": 10
            },
            "padding": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "20",
              "right": "20",
              "bottom": "20",
              "left": "20",
              "isLinked": true
            },
            "background_background": "classic",
            "background_color": "#FFFFFF",
            "border_border": "solid",
            "border_color": "#E7E3D9",
            "border_radius": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "16",
              "right": "16",
              "bottom": "16",
              "left": "16",
              "isLinked": true
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "000102a",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "title": "Task 3 \u2014 Deduplicate to One Row per Order",
                "header_size": "h3",
                "title_color": "#14151A"
              },
              "elements": []
            },
            {
              "id": "000102b",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "<p>Keep only the latest version of each order. orders is an append-only, event-sourced fact, so \"the current state of an order\" has to be derived by picking one row per business key out of every version that was ever written.</p><p>A naive GROUP BY order_id looks like it dedupes, but most engines (SQLite included) allow non-aggregated columns in the SELECT list that aren't in the GROUP BY, and silently return an arbitrary row's value for them. A correlated subquery on MAX(updated_at) doesn't fully solve it either: updated_at is NULL on an order's very first row, and at least one order has two rows sharing the exact same updated_at (a replayed webhook), so the tie has to be broken deterministically.</p><p>The reliable pattern is a window function: COALESCE(updated_at, order_ts) as the primary ordering key, order_version as an explicit tie-break, and ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY ...) to rank rows within each order, then filter to rn = 1.</p><p><span style='font-style:italic;'>Hint: Partition by order_id, order by the coalesced timestamp then version, keep rn = 1.</span></p>",
                "text_color": "#54575F"
              },
              "elements": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "000102f",
          "elType": "container",
          "isInner": true,
          "settings": {
            "flex_direction": "column",
            "flex_gap": {
              "unit": "px",
              "size": 10
            },
            "padding": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "20",
              "right": "20",
              "bottom": "20",
              "left": "20",
              "isLinked": true
            },
            "background_background": "classic",
            "background_color": "#FFFFFF",
            "border_border": "solid",
            "border_color": "#E7E3D9",
            "border_radius": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "16",
              "right": "16",
              "bottom": "16",
              "left": "16",
              "isLinked": true
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "000102d",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "title": "Task 4 \u2014 Fix the Join Instead of the Symptom",
                "header_size": "h3",
                "title_color": "#14151A"
              },
              "elements": []
            },
            {
              "id": "000102e",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "<p>Even with orders deduplicated, joining it straight to order_items and grouping is fragile, because that conflates two different problems into a single GROUP BY. Aggregating order_items to order grain first, one row per order_id with revenue already summed, turns the second join into a clean 1:1 merge.</p><p>Separately, one line item has a NULL unit_price. SUM() ignores NULLs rather than propagating them, so SUM(quantity * unit_price) silently drops that line item's contribution instead of failing loudly. Use COALESCE(unit_price, 0) to make the zero explicit, and add a has_missing_price flag.</p><p><span style='font-style:italic;'>Hint: Aggregate order_items to order grain in its own step before joining anything to it.</span></p>",
                "text_color": "#54575F"
              },
              "elements": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "0001032",
          "elType": "container",
          "isInner": true,
          "settings": {
            "flex_direction": "column",
            "flex_gap": {
              "unit": "px",
              "size": 10
            },
            "padding": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "20",
              "right": "20",
              "bottom": "20",
              "left": "20",
              "isLinked": true
            },
            "background_background": "classic",
            "background_color": "#FFFFFF",
            "border_border": "solid",
            "border_color": "#E7E3D9",
            "border_radius": {
              "unit": "px",
              "top": "16",
              "right": "16",
              "bottom": "16",
              "left": "16",
              "isLinked": true
            }
          },
          "elements": [
            {
              "id": "0001030",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "heading",
              "settings": {
                "title": "Task 5 \u2014 Assemble and Verify the Trusted Query",
                "header_size": "h3",
                "title_color": "#14151A"
              },
              "elements": []
            },
            {
              "id": "0001031",
              "elType": "widget",
              "widgetType": "text-editor",
              "settings": {
                "editor": "<p>Combine Tasks 3 and 4 into one query in starter/solution.sql returning exactly one row per order_id, with columns order_id, status, revenue, has_missing_price. Then run the automated check: python tests/test_solution.py. It checks required columns, one-row-per-order grain, and an exact match against the trusted revenue and status for every order, and exits non-zero if any check fails.</p>",
                "text_color": "#54575F"
              },
              "elements": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
